    "q-15331": {
      "dom_id": "step-q15331",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "A cinema hall can hold $120$ people. The price of each ticket is $$10.$ The cost of maintaining the cinema is made up of a fixed cost of $$400$ plus a cost of $$0.25$ per person. How many tickets should the cinema sell to maximize its profit?",
      "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ tickets, the cinema has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=10\\cdot x=10x\n$$\n\nand a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nC(x)={{400}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}\\,\\,+{{0.25x.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =10x-(400+0.25x) \\\\  & =9.75x-400.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 120.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=120$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<120.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=9.75.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ has no solutions.\n\nOur candidates are $x=0$ and $x=120.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =9.75(0)-400=-400 \\\\ P(120) & =9.75(120)-400=770\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the cinema will have a maximum profit level when it sells $120$ tickets.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
