    "q-87576": {
      "dom_id": "step-q87576",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "A daycare center can take care of between $10$ and $20$ children. The fee that parents must pay each month depends on the number of children enrolled in the center. If there are $10$ children enrolled, parents must pay $$40.$ On the other hand, if there are more than $10$ children enrolled, the price drops by $$2$ for each child. All the parents pay the same monthly fee.\n\nHow many children must enroll for the center to maximize its revenue?",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that if there are $10+x$ children enrolled, then the monthly fee is $40-2x.$ So, the total revenue is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(10+x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{number of children enrolled}}\\times \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{(40-2x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{monthly fee}} \\\\  & =(10+x)(40-2x) \\\\  & =400-20x+40x-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =400+20x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the center can only take care between $10$ and $20$ children, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & \\le x+10\\le 20 \\\\ 0 & \\le x\\le 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=10$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $0<x<10.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=20-4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 20-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =20 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=5$ lies in the interval $0<x<10.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=5$ does indeed give a maximum value of $R(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{R}^{″}(x)=-4<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=5$ is indeed a local maximum of $R(x).$ And since $R(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=5$ is a global maximum of $R(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the center must have $10+5=15$ children enrolled to maximize its revenue.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
