    "q-119056": {
      "dom_id": "step-q119056",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "A company spends $C(x)=0.01{x}^{2}+4x+680$ dollars to produce $x$ units of a certain product. The company sells the product for $$12$ per unit. Given that the company can produce at most $500$ units per month, how many units should the company produce per month to maximize its monthly profit?",
      "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per month, the company has a monthly revenue (income) of $R(x)=12\\cdot x=12x$ dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =12x-(0.01{x}^{2}+4x+680) \\\\  & =8x-0.01{x}^{2}-680.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 500.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<500.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=8-0.02x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 8-0.02x & =0 \\\\ x & =400\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=400$ lies in the interval $0<x<500.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=400$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-0.02<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=400$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=400$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the company will have a maximum monthly profit when it produces $400$ units per month.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
